// src/kernel/core/tty/stdin_drain.pdx — R17-M5 (#748)
// tty_stdin_drain: pump bytes from _uart_rx_ring through tty_process_input
// so cooked-mode line accumulation (echo, backspace, CR/LF, EOL) fires
// automatically from real IRQ-delivered UART input.
//
// Motivation (see design/kernel/r17-m5-636-shell-binary-seed.md §2 prereq #3
// and issue #748):
//
//   Real UART RX IRQ delivery (#666 landed) enqueues each RBR byte into
//   _uart_rx_ring via uart_rx_isr → uart_rx_enqueue. But _tty_line_buf
//   (the cooked-mode single-line accumulator drained by tty_read_try) is
//   populated only by direct calls to tty_process_input — never from the
//   ring. Bytes were arriving but sitting in the ring forever, so
//   sys_read(0, buf, N) on the TTY vnode blocked indefinitely.
//
//   This function closes that gap: it is the ring→line-buffer bridge.
//
// Placement rationale (per issue §"Design choice space" option 1):
//
//   Called from the slow path of tty_read (post-fast-path-miss), inside a
//   cli window that also brackets the subsequent set_waiter + sched_block
//   sequence. This gives a clean atomic "drain-then-check-then-block"
//   discipline that closes the classic wake-race:
//
//     Race without cli:  try→empty  →  IRQ enqueue+wake (no waiter set)
//                        →  set_waiter  →  sched_block  ⇒  sleep forever
//                                                          with byte ready.
//
//     With cli-around-drain-then-block: any IRQ that would enqueue is
//     deferred until sched_block flips IF back on inside the switch, and
//     the waiter is guaranteed set by then, so the wake fires correctly.
//
// Why NOT option 3 (in-ISR call to tty_process_input): tty_process_input
//   invokes uart_putc for echo, which spins on LSR THRE. Placing that spin
//   inside the RX ISR extends ISR runtime by potentially hundreds of
//   microseconds per byte, delaying every other IRQ (including the timer
//   IRQ that drives sched preemption). Issue #748 explicitly warns against
//   this ("dangerous — extends ISR runtime with cooked-mode work"), and
//   the design doc for #636 §2 pins option 1 as the recommended path.
//
// IRQ-safety note: this function runs with IF=0 (its callers are expected
//   to have cli'd). uart_rx_dequeue is SPSC-safe with concurrent
//   uart_rx_enqueue (from a re-entering ISR, which cannot occur under
//   IF=0 anyway on UP). tty_process_input mutates only _tty_line_buf /
//   _tty_line_head / _tty_line_complete, which no ISR touches under the
//   option-1 design. So the drain body is safe to interleave with (still-
//   deferred) IRQs; the surrounding cli exists purely to close the
//   set-waiter race, not to protect drain state.
//
// See design/kernel/r17-m5-636-shell-binary-seed.md and issue #748.

module StdinDrain = structure {
  // ==========================================================================
  // tty_stdin_drain() -> ()
  // ==========================================================================
  // Input:  (none)
  // Output: (none — rax undefined on return)
  //
  // Side effects (per drained byte):
  //   - one _uart_rx_ring dequeue (advances _uart_rx_tail by 1)
  //   - one tty_process_input(byte) call, which per its own contract:
  //       * echoes 1..3 bytes to COM1 THR via uart_putc,
  //       * appends 1 byte to _tty_line_buf (advances _tty_line_head),
  //       * sets _tty_line_complete = 1 iff the byte is '\n' / '\r',
  //       * emits BEL (0x07) on line-buffer overflow (drop-on-overflow).
  //
  // Side effects (empty ring): none — first uart_rx_dequeue returns the
  //   sentinel 0xFFFF and the loop exits immediately. Two-instruction
  //   fast path for the common "IRQ hasn't fired yet" case (dequeue +
  //   sentinel compare + jump — call sites see O(1) empty-ring cost).
  //
  // Termination: bounded by the ring capacity (RING_BYTES = 256 per
  //   uart_rx_ring.pdx:17). Each iteration consumes exactly one byte from
  //   the ring; no iteration re-enqueues. Under IF=0 on UP no concurrent
  //   producer can add bytes during the drain, so the upper bound is
  //   256 iterations even in the pathological case.
  //
  // Clobbers (SysV caller-save): rax, rcx, rdx, rdi, rsi, r8, r9, r10,
  //   r11 — union of uart_rx_dequeue's (rax, rcx, rdx, r9, r10),
  //   tty_process_input's (rax, rcx, rdx, r8), focus_get_keyboard's
  //   (rax, r10, r11), and kbd_event_deliver's (rax, rcx, rdx, rsi, r8..r11).
  //   Callee-save rbx is now saved and restored (see alignment note).
  //
  // Alignment: caller enters at rsp%16==8 per SysV (its own `call
  //   tty_stdin_drain` pushed 8). We make four families of nested calls
  //   (uart_rx_dequeue, tty_process_input, focus_get_keyboard,
  //   kbd_event_deliver — the last two via kbd_route_from_tty_byte) —
  //   each `call` pushes another 8, so nested callees enter at rsp%16==0,
  //   which is what they expect. Since R113.M3-012 (#2392) we save the
  //   drained byte across tty_process_input in callee-save rbx so it can
  //   be re-passed to kbd_route_from_tty_byte in the same iteration; the
  //   `push rbx` at entry (was `sub rsp, 8`) restores rsp%16==0 at the
  //   loop head symmetrically to the prior form, and the matching
  //   `pop rbx` at exit restores rsp%16==8 for our `ret`.
  //   Same alignment idiom used by uart_rx_isr (rx_isr.pdx:54) and the
  //   vops dispatcher (vops.pdx frozen at R16-M1-003 #572 correction).
  pub let tty_stdin_drain : () -> () !{sysreg, mem} @{} = fn () -> unsafe {
    effects: { sysreg, mem },
    capabilities: { },
    justification: "R17-M5 (#748) + R113.M3-012 (#2392): ring→line-buffer bridge, additionally routing each drained byte to the focused surface's per-surface keyboard event ring. Loops calling uart_rx_dequeue (returns u64 byte in low 8 bits or 0xFFFF sentinel per rx_ring.pdx:87-88), checks rax against 0xFF via `mov rcx, 0xFF; cmp rax, rcx; ja tsd_done` — 0xFFFF > 0xFF so `ja` fires exactly on the empty-sentinel and never on a real byte (bytes are 0..0xFF). On a real byte: (a) stash the byte in callee-save rbx (survives across both nested calls per SysV); (b) mov rdi, rbx; call tty_process_input — routes the byte through the cooked-mode line-discipline chain (echo via uart_putc, append via tty_line_append, translate CR→LF, backspace erase, BEL on overflow — see process_input.pdx); (c) mov rdi, rbx; call kbd_route_from_tty_byte — R113.M3-012 wire (graphics/kbd_route.pdx) that looks up focus_get_keyboard() and, if focused, enqueues (byte, mods=0, is_press=1) into the focused surface's per-surface u64 ring; unfocused boots (every current 14-mode QEMU boot) short-circuit inside the helper so the added cost is a null-focus load + compare per byte. `push rbx` at entry aligns rsp%16 to 0 for nested SysV calls (replaces the prior `sub rsp, 8` one-for-one — same 8-byte adjustment, but now used to preserve the byte across tty_process_input rather than to pad). Non-leaf; the only callee-save touched is rbx (saved and restored via push/pop). Called by tty_read's slow-path from inside a cli window: the drain runs with IF=0 so no concurrent uart_rx_enqueue can race with our uart_rx_dequeue, and the atomic drain-then-set-waiter-then-block sequence closes the classic condition-variable wake race. Called by (a) tty_read's slow path (post-#748) as the sole production caller; (b) #748's kernel_main witness (tty_stdin_bridge_witness) which enqueues a byte via uart_rx_enqueue then calls this drain and verifies _tty_line_buf state. Encoder: every mnemonic used has landed precedent — `push rbx` / `pop rbx` (process_input.pdx:35,92), `mov rbx, rax` (any), `call sym` (any), `mov rcx, imm8` (rx_ring.pdx:53), `cmp rax, rcx` (rx_ring.pdx:55), `ja label` (rx_ring.pdx:56), `mov rdi, rbx` (any). No new paideia-as forms. Zero encoder gaps. IRQ-safety review: (1) tty_process_input mutates _tty_line_buf/_tty_line_head/_tty_line_complete — no ISR touches these under the option-1 design. (2) uart_rx_dequeue's SPSC-safety with concurrent uart_rx_enqueue holds under cli by construction (no re-entry). (3) uart_putc inside tty_process_input spins on LSR THRE — bounded to ~87us per byte at 115200 baud; for interactive input this holds cli for at most ~1ms total per drain call. (4) kbd_route_from_tty_byte writes _kbd_events/_kbd_head/_kbd_seen but no ISR touches those under the current wire posture (the hid_process_report path is dormant behind unwired MSI-X). Audit: r17-m5-748-tty-stdin-bridge + design/kernel/kind-tui-canvas.md §R113.M3.",
    block: {
      // Align rsp%16 to 0 so nested SysV calls (uart_rx_dequeue,
      // tty_process_input, kbd_route_from_tty_byte) enter at
      // rsp%16 == 8 as ABI requires.  Caller's `call tty_stdin_drain`
      // pushed 8 → we entered at rsp%16 == 8; push rbx → 0; matching
      // pop rbx at exit restores.  rbx doubles as the drained-byte
      // parking slot across tty_process_input so the same byte can
      // then be handed to kbd_route_from_tty_byte in one iteration.
      push rbx;

    tsd_loop:
      // Pull one byte from the ring; rax = byte (0..0xFF) or 0xFFFF.
      call uart_rx_dequeue;

      // Empty check: any value > 0xFF cannot be a real byte, so it
      // must be the DEQUEUE_EMPTY sentinel (0xFFFF). Use `ja` so the
      // sentinel path is a single, mispredict-friendly branch.
      mov  rcx, 0xFF;
      cmp  rax, rcx;
      ja   tsd_done;

      // Park the drained byte in callee-save rbx so it survives the
      // tty_process_input call and can be re-passed to the R113.M3-012
      // route helper without a second dequeue.
      mov  rbx, rax;

      // Real byte — feed it through the cooked-mode router.
      mov  rdi, rbx;                       // SysV arg 0 = byte
      call tty_process_input;              // echo + append + \r→\n + BS + BEL

      // R113.M3-012 (#2392) wire: route the same byte to the focused
      // surface's per-surface kbd ring.  Best-effort — the helper
      // returns void and swallows both the FOCUS_NONE arm (no focus
      // set) and any KBD_ROUTE_* soft failure.
      mov  rdi, rbx;
      call kbd_route_from_tty_byte;

      jmp  tsd_loop;

    tsd_done:
      pop rbx;
      ret
    }
  }
}
